From 330abdc54cf94f96dfed7d95898ebc2357332bb0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: JohnnyC96 <54557697+JohnnyC96@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 13:57:41 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] VIF1: faster UNPACK per-vector loop, same results

processVIF1Data was the largest function on Outbreak's game thread (22%
of samples in J's Bar's busiest stretch). Line-level sampling put 59% of
it on the per-field mask/mode loop and 19% on the store into VU1 memory:
the loop re-read vif1_regs (mask, row, col, mode) for every field of
every vector, because the stores into VU1 memory may alias them, and it
divided by WL twice per vector.

The UNPACK body now:
- copies mask, ROW, COL and mode into locals once per UNPACK and writes
  ROW back once at the end (mode 2 accumulates into it);
- keeps the STCYCL position in counters (writeIndex % wl, / wl);
- handles the unmasked cases without the per-field loop: decoded data
  (plus ROW in modes 1/2), ROW for a fill cycle with no source read, the
  old lanes for an unhandled format; masked UNPACKs keep the per-field
  rules;
- decodes V2/V3/V4-32 with one copy.
Same rules, same raw-copy fallback for unhandled formats.

Test (ps2x, PS2Memory): 8 deterministic random VIF1 segments (every
UNPACK format, mask on/off, modes 0-3, STCYCL with cl >= wl and cl < wl,
TOPS-relative addresses, zero/sign extension, mixed with STCYCL, STMOD,
STMASK, STROW, STCOL, BASE, OFFSET); the hash of VU1 data memory plus
ROW/COL after each segment equals the hash recorded with the previous
interpreter. With the final ROW write-back removed it fails (and one
existing UNPACK test with it).

In the game (outbreak-modern hostprof, 45 s of J's Bar's busy stretch):
processVIF1Data 22.3% -> 6.8% of game-thread samples, game thread
41.5 -> 33.8 CPU-s (no longer saturated); the unpaced J's Bar check
53 s -> 47-48 s with the same capture ticks.
